0016 candidate (this file is exp-21): one function decides the mutable
layout, as the replacement for 0014.

Status 20.11: written, NOT BUILT, NOT MEASURED, and not applied to the
working tree.  It is an alternative to
patches/0014-turnip-mutable-no-list-tiling.patch, not a patch on top of it
- both define the compatibility-class enumerator, so this one does not
apply to a tree that carries 0014.  The graduation number is 0016: 0015 is
spoken for by ADR-0009 and numbers are not reused.  Applied with git apply,
reverted with -R, on a tree with 0001..0013 and 0014 off.

Why replace something that measures well.  What 0014 costs is that its rule
is written twice.  tu_image_init() synthesizes the class inline - its own
VkFormat[64], its own VkImageFormatListCreateInfo - while
tu6_mutable_format_list_forces_linear() does the same thing through
tu_mutable_format_list_or_class(); and the decision itself, the tail of
that function, is repeated in tu_image_init() as an inline copy of the
three predicates.  The copies are kept in agreement by hand, and once they
were not kept: 0009 moved NV12 out of the linear fallback in
tu_image_init() and left the NV12 answer of the shared function on the rule
from before it.  Nothing reached that line, because the query refuses
multi-planar formats earlier - which is the lucky part of that story, not
the design.  The shadowed fmt_list is a smaller cost of the same kind:
after the synthesis the name no longer means what it says, the truth is in
ubwc_list, and the perf_debug line reports "fmt list present" about an
image whose application passed none.

The shape.  tu6_mutable_format_list_forces_linear() is the one function
that answers "must this mutable image be linear", for tu_image_init() and
for the format query.  It builds the class itself and hands it to the layout
predicates only; tu6_mutable_format_list_ubwc_compatible() is handed the
list of the application.  That separation - not the helper - is what makes
the second variable unnecessary, and it is the seam exp-18 will stand on:
giving compression back to a no-list image takes the strict Vulkan class,
not this block one (journal 24.4), so the two questions must not meet in one
pointer.

Why this is a refactor and not a rule change.  The four early-outs of the
function are no-ops for the new caller: it is reached inside
!tu6_mutable_format_list_ubwc_compatible(), outside depth and stencil, after
the NV12 branch, and inside !ubwc_all_formats_compatible.  Two lines of it
do change, and both are about reachability rather than about an image:

  - NV12 now answers "not linear", which is what 0009 decided for the create
    path (its comment here said "fall back to linear always" and is
    corrected).  No answer moves: the other caller is the sparse block of
    the format query, and that refuses multi-planar formats before it asks,
    so the line was unreachable while still encoding the pre-0009 rule;

  - the class no longer reaches the UBWC predicate.  No answer moves here
    either: fd6_ubwc_compat_mode() has one row per format, so a class of two
    or more members is never compatible, and the single-member class that
    could reach that early-out cannot force linear by itself.

One question is left as 0014 left it, rather than settled here: a
VkImageFormatListCreateInfo with viewFormatCount == 0 is forwarded to the
predicates instead of being answered with the class, although the spec's
wording makes it the same question as passing no list.  The inventories
cannot tell the two forms apart - both are listn=0 - so nothing here can
measure the difference: open question 12 of docs/plan-next-checks.md.

The measured rule is untouched, so the gate starts with an equivalence run,
not with a new number: the inventory has to reproduce the imginv5 column of
results/imginv-3variants.txt (the tiled and ubwc counters, the 1631 MiB
linear residue), then the 94-row matrix with NL, XV, UBR, S, N read
separately - 0014 v2 is why a green counter is not a gate (ADR-0006).

Not a smaller diff, and it should be said plainly: 0014 is 5 hunks
+128/-5, this is 7 hunks +127/-16.  What it buys is one decision path
instead of two, and an exported surface that does not grow: 0014 adds
tu6_mutable_compat_class() to tu_formats.h, this adds nothing, and with
exp-21b the three predicate declarations 0004 and 0005 added go back to
being static.

Applying this file to the current stack asks no rebase of anything: it was
generated on the tree with 0001..0013 applied, passes git apply --check and
reverts clean.  What moves is where the stack is fragile.  The branch of
tu_image_init() that 0005, 0009, 0010 and 0014 each edited keeps one
decision site instead of two, so a future rebase is resolved in one place -
which is the point, and also the reason the equivalence run comes before
anything else: a shared site turns one mistake into one behaviour instead of
two that happen to agree.

